 4. 📝 Axios interview: Bob Woodward&#x27;s secrets 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Mike Allen interviews Bob Woodward at his Georgetown home last week. Photo: JaMar Jones for Axios Bob Woodward — out today with " Secrets, " his memoir of 55 years of reporting for The Washington Post and 24 cinematic bestsellers , covering 10 presidents — told me in an interview for "The Axios Show" that the book exposes some confidential sources who are now dead because he feels a duty to history.

 Why it matters: Woodward, 83, is a double Pulitzer winner whose Watergate investigative reporting with Carl Bernstein became a book and movie, "All the President's Men," that inspired generations of would-be reporters, including me. But no journalism school teaches students they can out " secret sources " who are dead. "It's history," Woodward told me in the Georgetown home where so many sources have spilled to him, administration after administration, decade after decade. He explained that in many cases, the sources were widely known anyway or appeared in other files and records that have become public. He said some living sources gave him permission to be named in the book.

 "Look at the histories that come out — the Adams-Jefferson letters , which were very secret. But when they come out, historians rejoice. We now see what they were saying to each other ... I'm for history on occasions like that." 🎬 More from our interview, which you can watch here: 

 Axios: "You're still an associate editor of The Washington Post, the paper you helped make world-famous, a place where we were colleagues. ... Did Jeff Bezos put The Washington Post's reputation and future at risk?"

 Woodward: "Yes, certainly. I mean, he's the owner and to show up at Trump's inaugural, voluntarily, in a very visible way, which is not just: 'Oh, I'm going to the movies.' It's: 'I am blessing and endorsing this man.' And I remember seeing it and just feeling sick to my stomach. I knew Katharine Graham , the owner of the Post during Watergate, would never do anything like that." Axios: "When I first walked into The Post as a young Metro reporter [in 1997] — I was hired to cover the Alexandria City Council — I walked into that newsroom on 15th Street, and it was like walking onto Lambeau Field. It was The Show. The Post has shriveled since then."

 Woodward: "Shriveled is the wrong word. I mean, there have been some valleys. There's no question about that." Axios: What was Joe Biden's fatal flaw?

 Woodward: "Being president." Full episode ... Get "Secrets." 

 
 
